## Flexible flow — explore first, then let the user pick

When the question is "what should we benchmark here", do not commit to a
model or flags before seeing what is live. Run the discovery script, report
the table, and stop for direction:

```bash
python3 scripts/explore_engines.py              # probe default endpoints
python3 scripts/explore_engines.py --endpoint http://127.0.0.1:8100/v1
python3 scripts/explore_engines.py --json       # machine-readable
```

It probes Studio llama-swap (:9292), MacBook llama-swap
(100.68.81.83:8999), and Splash on :8100 on both hosts (2s timeout each),
lists the models each serves, flags Qwen3.8-family models, and prints the
tokenizer for any it knows. Then STOP: report the table and let the user say
"bench those" or redirect to a different model/engine/shape. The default
when the user just says go is the Qwen3.8-family set across whatever engines
are UP.

`benchy_interactive.py` remains the entry point for a committed measurement
with a known target; `explore_engines.py` is the entry point for an open
"what's available" question. For a cross-engine comparison, run the SAME
llama-benchy command with different `--base-url` values and keep every other
flag identical — that is the only way engine numbers are comparable.

## sushi reasoning benchmark (`scripts/sushi-reasoning-bench.py`)

Purpose: paired A/B on how thinking affects TTFT and tok/s for the `sushi`
model (MLX sushi engine, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next 125B-A6B 2bpw) served through
llama-swap on :9292. Arms: A=thinking off (ctk enable_thinking=false),
B=thinking on default, C=B + TOP-LEVEL `reasoning_budget_tokens=N` (--budget).
Run `ab --arms A,B --pairs 10 --json <path>`; `ab --arms B,C --budget 128`;
`noise --arm A --reps 8` for floor.

Verified server behavior (2026-10-07, empirical, all on the live endpoint,
temp=0 sheep prompt unless noted):
- Throttle: TOP-LEVEL `reasoning_budget_tokens` is a real hard cap enforced
  at decode (64->329c, 128->537c, 256->1073c, uncapped->2402c think). It is
  IGNORED inside chat_template_kwargs (ctk placement: 2402c everywhere) —
  the earlier "no throttle exists" conclusion was wrong; the field name was
  right, the placement was not.
- TOP-LEVEL `reasoning_effort` uses sushi's own per-arch table (this arch:
  off/low/medium/xhigh; 'minimal'/'high' 400-rejected). Traps: low/none/
  absent all render IDENTICALLY (default thinking); medium = longer (2822c,
  prompt 68); xhigh AND the string 'off' BOTH force deep thinking that runs
  until max_tokens (7670c think, 0 answer, finish=length, prompt 110) —
  'off' is NOT a disable. Disabling thinking = ctk {enable_thinking: False}
  (verified 0 think, full answer). Cap + effort combine: low+128->537c,
  low+512->1971c, xhigh+128->690c, off+128->688c, off+512->2046c.
- ctk-only `reasoning_effort` (all six OpenAI values) is silently IGNORED —
  byte-identical output. That, not a missing feature, is why the six-value
  sweep looked like effort was dead.
- Engine flag `sushi --reasoning-budget <n>` exists in --help but is
  serve-time default only; request-level cap above works without relaunch.
- Request-level `think_penalty <f>` works (lowers logits of overthinking
  markers inside reasoning span): 1.0 → 1506→2318c at same seed (thinks MORE).
- Streaming: usage/timings arrive on the FINAL chunk(s) with
  stream_options.include_usage; merge across chunks. completion_tokens
  includes reasoning; completion_tokens_details.reasoning_tokens is the split.
- Temporal anchoring (32GB mmapped ngram table): same prompt+seed after the
  15-min idle unload replays BYTE-IDENTICAL thinking content. Does not defeat
  the paired design; do NOT treat identical think_chars across reps as
  "determinism" — it's cache replay. temp=1 real variance: think 434-1052
  reasoning tokens across seeds on the same prompt.
- llama-swap `groups.sushi` runs `sushi serve` with `--mtp`, no
  --reasoning-budget; `ttl: 900` = unload after 15 min idle.

Benchmark design: paired interleaved (arms alternate within each pair, pair
order shuffled) cancels thermal drift. Verdicts must clear p<0.05 AND the
minimum detectable effect for the pair count; everything else INCONCLUSIVE.

Main run (temp=0, max_tokens=2048, 10 pairs, seed 5, A vs B): thinking adds
~918 reasoning tokens, TTFT-to-answer grows ~0.61s -> ~8-30s (answer appears
only after the thinking phase), answer span shrinks 2910->494 tokens at the
2048 cap, e2e tok/s drops ~50->24, decode tok/s on visible text roughly flat
(per-token rate barely slower; cost is the preamble + shared token budget).
Budget-cap confirmation (temp=0, max_tokens=2048, seed 5, 10 pairs, B vs
C@128): think 600->135 tokens; ttft_answer 8456->2404 ms (cap recovers 3.5x
to-answer latency, p<0.0001); tok_s_e2e 35.5->47.4 (+25%); total wall 13.7->9.6s;
answer_tokens 486->455 (short answers, ~7% loss). NOTE: tok_s_answer and
tok_s_decode read HIGHER for B (92.6/83.1 vs 63.3/65.8) — that's a division
artifact, not a per-token speed win: capped-arm answers ramble longer per
token (temp=0 anchoring replays different rollouts), so the shorter-answer
arm's span divides into fewer tok/s. The honest per-token rate is ~80-90
tok/s decode at the 2048-token span for both arms; the throughput gains of
capping are WALL-CLOCK (fewer tokens total), not rate.
Curve shape at temp=0 (uncapped=600t think): budget 64c/128c/256c/512c ->
think ~250/135/450/490t at the 2048 cap. Caveats: truncation at max_tokens
cuts thinking arms mid-answer; long-answer prompts under a token cap mix
length effects into tok/s — report with pairs and MDE, not single numbers.
